     The window menu's PPrriinntt entry opens the print dialog: scale,
     wide, and the three verbs - PPrriinntt spools the drawing through
     velxport into llpprr, PPrreevviieeww opens a window (velprev) drawing
     the printable extent through the SAME renderer that feeds
     the printer, so what it shows is what the paper gets.  The
     SSeeaarrcchh entry searches designators, values, text and net
     names for a substring, selects the hit and pans it to
     centre.  NNeexxtt and PPrreevv walk the object list either way round
     from the current selection, wrapping; with nothing selected
     they open at the first and the last object respectively.
     Matching ignores case unless MMaattcchh ccaassee is ticked.  The pat-
     tern, the direction and the box are remembered, so aa on the
     canvas (or a second SSeeaarrcchh) steps to the next hit without
     the dialog.  Its SShheeeettss button lists the numbered set and
     jumps by number.  The DDuupp toolbar cell with a live selection
     opens the ARRAY dialog (columns, rows, pitch) - pin headers
     and terminal strips in one commit; the armed Dup tool still
     copies single objects click by click.  While a wire, line,
     connector or dimension drag is up, the status line's coor-
     dinate cell shows the run's LENGTH times the sheet unit -
     measuring without committing a dimension.  Zoom reaches down
     to 2 px per unit - the whole A4 sheet in one window - where
     the grid thins to every fourth unit.  A text, shape label or
     dimension override may run to 39 characters; the value of a
     symbol stays 15.  Cut, Copy and Paste (window menu) go
     through the system clipboard AS TEXT in the drawing format -
     paste a fragment into another Vellum, or into zzeeddiitt as text.
     A middle CLICK pastes at the pointer (the selection, else
     the clipboard); a middle DRAG pans the sheet.  Persistent
     groups select, move, copy and delete as one until ungrouped.
     When the drawing is modified, an autosave writes _f_i_l_e.bbaakk
     every five minutes.

     vveelllluumm +_x,_y _f_i_l_e._d opens with the view CENTRED on that grid
     point and the object there selected.  Every asking mode
     prints its hits as _f_i_l_e: ... _a_t _x,_y, so this hands a report
     straight back to the board: vveelllluumm -wwhheerree TTOODDOO *.dd stops
     being a report and becomes a work list.

     The yy key turns the SELECTION into a library stencil - the
     editor's face of vveelllluumm -mmkkssyymm. It asks for the stencil's
     code, its designator prefix, the library file to append to,
     and the scale, then writes the selection out as an ordinary
     drawing and runs the exporter on it, so a stencil made here
     and one made from make(1) are the same bytes.  The NN net-
     name markers in the selection become the PINS, in z-order;
     pin types are set afterwards in ssyymmeeddiitt, because a type is a
     property of the library and a guessed one builds a wrong
     ERC.  A running vveelllluumm reads its libraries only at start-up,
     so the new stencil appears in the palette next time.


