     qquuaarrtteerr-ggrriidd coordinates.  LL and WW become ss segments, a BB
     box its four, a PP polyline its n-1, CC a circle, AA an arc
     angles and all, and a TT whose text is ONE CHARACTER a tt
     glyph.  Everything else - shapes, connectors, dimensions,
     nested symbols and multi-character text - is SKIPPED AND
     COUNTED on stderr, because a silently half-converted stencil
     is a part that draws wrong forever.

     The PINS come from the NN net-name markers, in the order they
     appear in the file (which is the z-order, and which -ssppiiccee
     reads when a library does not name its pins); the net name
     becomes the pin name, and an NN called - is a pin with no
     name.  Pin TYPES come from -ttyyppee _N_A_M_E=_t on the command line
     and never from the drawing: a type is a property of the
     LIBRARY, and a guessed type builds a wrong ERC.

     Coordinates are exact by construction.  A stencil is in
     quarter-grid units and a drawing in whole ones, so the con-
     version is x4 and every pin lands on a multiple of 4 - which
     is what the loader wants.  -ssccaallee _n divides instead, for a
     stencil sketched n times oversize so its detail was drawable
     at all; at -ssccaallee 44 a drawing unit IS a quarter unit and a
     hand-cut stencil's detail is available.  -oorrgg _x,_y names the
     grid point that becomes (0,0); the default is the FIRST PIN,
     because a stencil's origin is where it snaps (a sketch with
     no NN markers at all falls back to the top-left of its print-
     able extent, and is told on stderr that it has no pins).

     vveelllluumm -ssyymmcchheecckk proves the libraries the way -cchheecckk proves
     the drawings, and it matters more: a wrong stencil is wrong
     in EVERY drawing.  One finding per line as _l_i_b._s_y_m:  _m_e_s-
     _s_a_g_e, the exit status the finding count capped at 254 - the
     -cchheecckk shape exactly, so make(1) gates on it the same way.
     It reports a code defined twice, a code defined in two of
     the libraries given, a symbol with no geometry, a designator
     prefix with no pins, a pin off a whole unit, a pin name that
     repeats, a pin left untyped in a library that types the
     others, and - the two that nothing else can say - pins past
     the eight the loader keeps, and symbols the loader DROPPED
     because a pool filled.  Both of those the loader has always
     done silently, and the load order then decides which stencil
     a drawing gets.

     Together with make(1) that changes what a library IS, and
     costs no code at all:

                  STENCILS = pump.d valve.d tank.d
                  pid.sym: $(STENCILS)
                            rm -f $@
                            $(V) -mksym PUMP -pfx P pump.d >> $@
                            $(V) -mksym VALVE -pfx V valve.d >> $@
                            $(V) -mksym TANK -pfx TK tank.d >> $@
                            $(V) -symcheck $@
                  card: pid.sym ; $(V) -symsheet pid.sym | $(V) -ps -fit -



